# etude-ikj3 implementation report

Worker assignment: `gpt-5.6-terra`, high reasoning, as supplied by the
launcher. This report does not claim an independently observable runtime model
identifier.

## Delivered behavior

- Forward replay resolves the exact recorded stage name first. If that misses,
  it removes exactly one final engine-style retry suffix `.rN`, only for a
  canonical decimal `N >= 2`, and resolves that exact base stage.
- Literal names (including `plan.r2`) retain precedence; `plan.r2.r3` can
  resolve the literal `plan.r2` stage. `.r1`, zero/leading-zero, signed,
  non-decimal, and missing-base forms fail with the normal missing-stage error.
- `internal/liverun.ReplayForward` resolves every runner before creating its
  checkout or invoking a runner. It retains manifest order, stage names,
  producer data, and recorded input artifacts.
- The explicit `--runner` forward override retains its all-stage behavior.
- `docs/run.md` now documents retry lookup, recorded-input semantics,
  preflight, and the existing caller-workspace SHA exception accurately.

## Tests added

- Default workflow/registry tests use real shell adapters, not `forwardRunner`:
  mixed plan/review/literal bindings; original `review`, `review.r2`, and
  `review.r3` with distinct recorded inputs; literal `plan.r2`; and nested
  `plan.r2.r3`.
- Lookup-table coverage covers exact precedence, malformed suffixes, `r1`,
  zero and leading-zero forms, signed forms, and a missing base.
- Preflight tests establish resolution of every stage before execution and no
  invocation after a later resolution failure. CLI tests also establish no
  shell side effect or partial stdout for invalid default lookup.

## Documentation assessment

Updated `docs/run.md`, the authoritative forward-replay guide. I reviewed
`docs/replay.md`; it describes the single-stage command and needed no forward
replay contract change. During independent QA, I corrected adjacent stale SHA
guidance: hermetic stages must match the original checkout SHA; caller-workspace
stages may record their clean post-run SHA; differing hermetic SHAs are
rejected rather than recoverable through single-stage replay.

